# [VCON](../wg/vcon.html)

## Summary

The VCON Working Group met at IETF 127 to review the working group charter update, discuss major architectural updates to the core vCon specification (`draft-ietf-vcon-vcon-core`), receive status updates on existing working group drafts, and evaluate proposals related to AI agent transactions, lifecycle/lawful basis tracking, and real-time AI preferences.

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Charter Updates
* Chris Wendt presented the updated working group charter diff, incorporating expanded scope for AI agents and relevant use cases.
* Thomas McCarthy-Howe objected to the omission of the privacy section in the revised charter draft, emphasizing that privacy drives core functionality such as redaction, lifecycle, and consent tracking. Chris Wendt agreed that the privacy section must remain in the charter.

### Core Specification Updates (`draft-ietf-vcon-vcon-core`)
Dan Petrie presented updates and open issues from draft-05 in the presentation [Petrie vCon-core](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-vcon-petrie-vcon-core-00):

* **Architectural Changes in draft-05:**
  * Added a `session` object to explicitly group and scope dialogues.
  * Elevated party history events to independent top-level `event` objects (capturing join, drop, DTMF, button presses, etc.).
  * Replaced transfer dialogues with explicit transfer events (blind transfer, consult start, consultative transfer, transfer abandoned).
  * Introduced `channel_contributors` events to track active speakers/recording channels over time in multi-channel recordings.
  * Differentiated party roles: dialogue parties represent contributors, while session parties represent receivers.
  * Clarified redaction labeling and mechanisms.

* **Hackathon & Open Issues Discussion:**
  * **Redaction Markers & Strings:** Dan Petrie proposed using full marker strings rather than integer indices in `redacted_string`. Jonathan Rosenberg agreed, suggesting a maximum character length limit (e.g., 255 characters) to prevent prompt injection vectors. Jonathan Lennox noted that marker strings must be syntactically distinct from literal text.
  * **Redacted Segments:** Agreed to change segment positioning parameters to use start time and duration instead of start and end times.
  * **Message Threading:** Discussed supporting email headers (`In-Reply-To`, `References`). Rowan Naylor (Rohan Mahy) suggested abstracting message ID types across protocols (SMTP, MIMI).
  * **Media Type Folding:** Agreed to require header parameters to be unfolded into single lines by senders, while recommending that implementations remain tolerant of folded input.
  * **Session Requirement Rules:** Debate took place between Dan Petrie, Jonathan Rosenberg, Thomas McCarthy-Howe, and Ben Curtis regarding whether `session` objects should be mandatory. Jonathan Rosenberg suggested allowing sessions without start times/durations for future or unstarted calls, while Thomas McCarthy-Howe noted imported historical data may lack session details.
  * **IANA Registries:** Discussed single-table structure versus selective registration. Rowan Naylor noted that not every extensible element requires an IANA registry if conflict potential is low.
  * **Content Hash & Unstable URLs:** Thomas McCarthy-Howe noted that external links (such as YouTube recordings of IETF meetings) lack stable content hashes. Dan Petrie proposed making content hashes optional when media is not yet finalized or stable.

### Document Status Overview
* `draft-ietf-vcon-overview`: Thomas McCarthy-Howe noted this draft needs an update to incorporate the new session and event structures introduced in `draft-ietf-vcon-vcon-core` -05.
* `draft-ietf-vcon-privacy-primer`: Thomas McCarthy-Howe reported that the draft has been updated based on privacy review feedback and is ready for broader review.
* `draft-ietf-vcon-cc-extension`: Dan Petrie indicated the draft will be updated to align CDDL and JSON schemas with `draft-ietf-vcon-vcon-core` -05.

### External References for AI Agent Transactions
Francesco Marino Ni Moretto presented [How does a vCon point to an order? AI agent purchases and order records](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-vcon-how-does-a-vcon-point-to-an-order-ai-agent-purchases-and-order-records-00):
* Explored how a vCon can reference external order or payment records generated by AI agent transactions.
* Analyzed options including core attachments, tool call outputs, or extension points.
* Dan Petrie and Thomas McCarthy-Howe commented that while specialized transaction extensions are in line with vCon's future direction, external business events can currently be handled via vCon attachments or analysis references.

### Agent Communication & Audit Drafts
* Hank Birkholz provided an update on individual work defining verifiable agent communication records and aligning CDDL extension points with `draft-ietf-vcon-vcon-core`.
* Dan Petrie highlighted related drafts covering audit requirements and tool invocation extensions, agreeing to collaborate with Hank Birkholz to consolidate overlapping concepts.

### Lifecycle and Lawful Basis
Thomas McCarthy-Howe presented [Lifecycle and Lawful Basis](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-vcon-lifecycle-and-lawful-basis-00):
* Outlined two draft proposals:
  * **Lawful Basis:** An extension detailing granular consent, lawful basis categories (GDPR aligned), duration, and pointers to verification ledgers.
  * **Lifecycle:** Integration with SCITT ledgers to track life cycle events (created, consent accepted, consent revoked, vCon purged) across distributed copies.
* Requested Working Group adoption for both drafts.

### AI Preferences for Real-Time Protocol Bindings
Altanai B presented [AI Preferences for Real-Time Protocol Bindings - Altanai B](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-vcon-ai-preferences-for-real-time-protocol-bindings-altanai-b-00):
* Proposed signaling real-time user privacy preferences (e.g., do not transcribe, do not record, do not use for AI training) via SIP/SDP headers or PASSPorT tokens.
* Thomas McCarthy-Howe and Dan Petrie supported integrating these signaling preferences into vCon consent and lawful basis structures.

